# Subaward and Subrecipient Monitoring Policy

**{{ org_name }}** — a 501(c)(3) nonprofit research institute incorporated in {{ state }}
EIN: {{ ein }}

## Section 1 — Purpose

This policy establishes procedures for issuing subawards and monitoring subrecipients when {{ org_name }} ("the Organization") acts as a pass-through entity under federal awards. It ensures compliance with the Uniform Guidance at 2 CFR 200.331–200.333 and provides a practical framework appropriate for a small nonprofit research institute.

## Section 2 — Scope

This policy applies to all subaward relationships in which the Organization passes federal funds to another entity (subrecipient) to carry out a portion of a federally funded program or project. It applies to all directors, officers, employees, and agents involved in proposing, negotiating, issuing, or managing subawards.

## Section 3 — Subrecipient vs. Contractor Determination

Before issuing any agreement, the Organization must determine whether the relationship is a subaward to a subrecipient or a procurement contract with a contractor, using the criteria in 2 CFR 200.331.

**Subrecipient characteristics:**

- Determines who is eligible to receive program benefits;
- Has its performance measured against the objectives of the federal program;
- Has responsibility for programmatic decision-making;
- Uses the federal funds to carry out a program of the organization, as opposed to providing goods or services for the pass-through entity's program.

**Contractor characteristics:**

- Provides goods or services within normal business operations;
- Provides similar goods or services to many different purchasers;
- Operates in a competitive environment;
- Provides ancillary goods or services that are not central to the federal program's objectives.

The Executive Director shall document the determination in writing before executing the agreement. When the substance of the relationship is ambiguous, the Organization shall use judgment based on the totality of circumstances and default to treating the entity as a subrecipient when in doubt.

## Section 4 — Pre-Award Risk Assessment

Before issuing a subaward, the Organization shall evaluate the prospective subrecipient's risk of noncompliance, considering at a minimum:

1. **Financial stability** — review of recent financial statements or Form 990;
2. **Management systems** — adequacy of the subrecipient's accounting system, internal controls, and ability to segregate federal funds;
3. **Audit history** — results of prior Single Audits (if applicable), including whether findings were resolved timely;
4. **Prior performance** — track record on previous subawards with the Organization or other federal pass-through entities;
5. **Proposed project capability** — technical capacity and qualified personnel to perform the scope of work.

The risk assessment shall be documented and retained in the subaward file. The assessed risk level (standard or higher) shall determine the intensity of monitoring activities described in Section 6.

## Section 5 — Required Subaward Content

Every subaward agreement shall include the information required by 2 CFR 200.332(a), including but not limited to:

| Element | Description |
|---------|-------------|
| Federal award identification | FAIN, federal award date, and name of the federal awarding agency |
| CFDA information | CFDA number and name for each program under which the subaward is made |
| Subaward period of performance | Start and end dates |
| Budget and amount obligated | Total amount of the subaward and amount obligated at the time of execution |
| Indirect cost rate | The subrecipient's federally negotiated rate, or the 15% de minimis rate per 2 CFR 200.414(f) if no negotiated rate exists |
| Compliance requirements | Applicable federal statutes, regulations, and award terms that the subrecipient must follow |
| Reporting requirements | Financial and programmatic reports, their frequency (no less than annually), and due dates |
| Audit requirements | Notification that subrecipients spending $1,000,000 or more in federal funds during their fiscal year must have a Single Audit per 2 CFR 200 Subpart F |
| Access to records | Right of the Organization, the federal awarding agency, the Comptroller General, and their authorized representatives to access subrecipient records |
| Remedies for noncompliance | Reference to the remedies available under Section 8 of this policy |
| Closeout requirements | Deliverables and reports due at subaward completion |

## Section 6 — Monitoring Activities

### 6.1 Standard Monitoring (All Subrecipients)

The Organization shall, at a minimum:

- **Review financial reports** submitted by the subrecipient for each reporting period, verifying that expenditures are allowable, allocable, and within the approved budget;
- **Review programmatic reports** to confirm that the subrecipient is making adequate progress toward project objectives;
- **Verify audit compliance** — confirm annually whether the subrecipient is subject to the Single Audit requirement and, if so, review audit results via the Federal Audit Clearinghouse;
- **Follow up on audit findings** — if the subrecipient's Single Audit discloses findings related to the Organization's subaward, issue a management decision within **six months** of acceptance of the audit report (per 2 CFR 200.332(d));
- **Maintain regular communication** with subrecipient project leads to identify issues early.

### 6.2 Enhanced Monitoring (Higher-Risk Subrecipients)

For subrecipients assessed as higher risk during the pre-award review, the Organization shall apply additional monitoring, which may include:

- More frequent financial or programmatic reporting (e.g., monthly instead of quarterly);
- **On-site reviews** of the subrecipient's financial and programmatic records, internal controls, and compliance with subaward terms;
- Requiring detailed supporting documentation with invoices (e.g., timesheets, receipts);
- Limiting the subaward to a shorter period with renewal contingent on satisfactory performance;
- Requiring the subrecipient to obtain technical or management assistance.

The Executive Director shall determine the specific enhanced measures based on the nature and degree of risk identified.

## Section 7 — Flow-Down of Federal Requirements

The Organization shall ensure that every subaward includes, or incorporates by reference, all applicable requirements from the federal award, including:

- Terms and conditions of the prime award that apply to the subrecipient's scope of work;
- Applicable provisions of 2 CFR Part 200, including cost principles and audit requirements;
- Any agency-specific regulations (e.g., 45 CFR Part 75 for HHS awards, 2 CFR Part 910 for DOE);
- Requirements related to FFATA reporting (if applicable), debarment and suspension verification, and lobbying restrictions.

The subrecipient shall not be held to requirements that are not included in or referenced by the subaward agreement.

## Section 8 — Remedies for Noncompliance

If the Organization determines that a subrecipient has failed to comply with the terms of the subaward or applicable federal requirements, the Organization may take one or more of the following actions, in proportion to the severity of the noncompliance:

1. **Provide additional technical assistance** or training to bring the subrecipient into compliance;
2. **Require a corrective action plan** with specific milestones and deadlines;
3. **Withhold payment** until the subrecipient demonstrates compliance;
4. **Disallow all or part of the cost** of the activity or action not in compliance;
5. **Wholly or partly suspend** the subaward pending corrective action;
6. **Terminate the subaward** in accordance with 2 CFR 200.340.

The Organization shall document the basis for any remedy imposed and provide the subrecipient with written notice and a reasonable opportunity to respond before taking adverse action (except where immediate action is necessary to protect federal interests).

## Section 9 — Audit Requirements

Subrecipients that expend **$1,000,000 or more** in federal awards during their fiscal year must have a Single Audit conducted in accordance with 2 CFR 200 Subpart F. The Organization shall:

- Notify subrecipients of this requirement in the subaward agreement;
- Verify compliance annually by reviewing the Federal Audit Clearinghouse or requesting a copy of the audit report;
- Review audit findings related to the Organization's subaward and issue management decisions within six months;
- Consider the results of the subrecipient's audit when assessing risk for subsequent awards.

Subrecipients not subject to the Single Audit requirement remain subject to the monitoring procedures in Section 6 and may be required to make financial records available for review upon request.

## Section 10 — Record Retention

All subaward documentation — including the executed agreement, risk assessments, monitoring correspondence, financial and programmatic reports, audit reviews, management decisions, and any remedial actions — shall be retained for a minimum of **three years** from the date of submission of the final expenditure report for the federal award under which the subaward was made, in accordance with 2 CFR 200.334.

If any litigation, claim, audit, or negotiation involving the records is started before the end of the three-year period, the records shall be retained until the matter is fully resolved. Records shall be stored and managed in accordance with the Organization's Document Retention and Destruction Policy.

## Section 11 — Responsibilities

| Role | Responsibilities |
|------|-----------------|
| **Executive Director** | Approve subrecipient vs. contractor determinations; authorize subaward issuance; determine monitoring intensity; approve remedies for noncompliance; ensure policy compliance. |
| **PI / Project Director** | Conduct or contribute to pre-award risk assessments; monitor subrecipient programmatic performance; review technical deliverables; flag concerns to the Executive Director. |
| **Fiscal custodian** | Review subrecipient financial reports and invoices; track expenditures against subaward budgets; verify Single Audit compliance; process payments; maintain subaward financial records. |
| **Board of Directors** | Provide oversight of subaward activity through regular financial reporting and the annual audit; review this policy annually. |

## Section 12 — Annual Review

The Board of Directors shall review this policy at least annually, typically at the Q1 board meeting, to ensure it remains consistent with current federal regulations and sponsor requirements. The date of each review shall be recorded in the board minutes.

